From b17b9367def484a30c09d5e51602198d9fda86bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 21:51:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: sun6i-isp: Make the parameter and table requests
 rising edges

Reading FE_CTRL back while streaming, twelve seconds after the one
parameters buffer that asked for a table reload, showed LENS_UPDATE,
GAMMA_UPDATE and DRC_UPDATE all still set. Nothing clears them: not the
hardware, which would have to for the bit to mean anything a second
time, and not this driver, which only ever ORs them in. Once high they
stayed high, so a request could never be made again.

The vendor kernel treats these as edges. Its frame done handler clears
PARA_READY, reprograms the load buffer and sets PARA_READY again, an
explicit low to high transition per frame, and its table update helper
writes each of the four update bits from a mask rather than ORing, so a
bit not being asked for is actively cleared.

Do the same: drop the request bits before raising them, so that every
request is a transition rather than a write of a value that is already
there. FE_CTRL now reads 0x06 where it read 0x76.

This does not make the lens shading, the gamma curve or the dynamic
range compression work -- all three were measured again afterwards and
are still bit for bit no-ops, so whatever holds them is elsewhere, and
is probably what also holds the colour matrix and the contrast, which
are register driven and were never explained by this. It is a fix for
the request bits, which were wrong on their own terms.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 .../staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp.c
index 57fe913d12ca..e42cd50a7d0b 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp.c
@@ -65,11 +65,35 @@ void sun6i_isp_load_write(struct sun6i_isp_device *isp_dev, u32 offset,
  * - state complete: to indicate that the state update was applied.
  */
 
+/*
+ * The request bits are edges, not levels. Nothing in the hardware clears
+ * them once a request has been served, so a driver that only ever sets
+ * them leaves them high and the next request never becomes a transition.
+ * Clear them here, against the value about to be written, so that setting
+ * them below is always a rising edge.
+ */
+static void sun6i_isp_state_arm(struct sun6i_isp_device *isp_dev)
+{
+	struct regmap *regmap = isp_dev->regmap;
+	u32 value;
+
+	regmap_read(regmap, SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_REG, &value);
+	value &= ~(SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_PARA_READY |
+		   SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_LUT_UPDATE |
+		   SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_LENS_UPDATE |
+		   SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_GAMMA_UPDATE |
+		   SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_DRC_UPDATE |
+		   SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_DISC_UPDATE);
+	regmap_write(regmap, SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_REG, value);
+}
+
 static void sun6i_isp_state_ready(struct sun6i_isp_device *isp_dev)
 {
 	struct regmap *regmap = isp_dev->regmap;
 	u32 value;
 
+	sun6i_isp_state_arm(isp_dev);
+
 	regmap_read(regmap, SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_REG, &value);
 	value |= SUN6I_ISP_FE_CTRL_PARA_READY;
 	value |= sun6i_isp_params_table_update_take(isp_dev);
-- 
